4.2 Data Bus Architecture

The City‑wide Data Bus (CDB) is implemented as a dual‑plane publish/subscribe system:

· Plane A (TTEthernet, 1 kHz deterministic):
  · Carries time‑critical messages: reactor telemetry, attitude control commands, emergency alerts.
  · Uses Time‑Triggered schedules pre‑computed and validated offline.
  · Subscribers: QNI, Photonic AI, Triad (protective), Booster.
· Plane B (10 GbE, Kafka):
  · Carries non‑deterministic data: video streams, sensor history, digital twin updates, logs.
  · Retains last 24 h of all topics for replay and analysis.
  · Subscribers: Fusion Engine, Engineering Optimiser, Triad (strategic), ground downlink.

4.3 Timing and Synchronisation

· Global Time Reference: GPS‑disciplined oscillator + chip‑scale atomic clock, distributed via IEEE 802.1AS (gPTP) over TTEthernet.
· Synchronous Execution: All real‑time control loops (reactor, attitude, docking) run at fixed frequencies (1 kHz, 10 kHz) triggered by hardware timers synchronised to the global time.
· Asynchronous Fusion: The Sensor Fusion Engine runs asynchronously, ingesting the latest available data and publishing a new world model every 100 ms.

