7. Key Component Implementation Details

7.1 Triad AI – Ethical Constraint Enforcement

Implementation:

· Each Triad node runs an ethical reasoner based on a hybrid of deontic logic and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF).
· The reasoner is implemented as a neural‑symbolic system: a transformer encodes the proposed action and current context; the output is passed through a set of formally verified logical constraints (e.g., “no discrimination”, “right to privacy”).
· Training: RLHF is performed via a secure, privacy‑preserving federated learning system. Residents can optionally contribute feedback; data is aggregated with differential privacy (ε=2.0).

Ethical Audit API (simplified protobuf):

```protobuf
message Decision {
  string layer_id = 1;
  string action_id = 2;
  map<string, Value> parameters = 3;
  WorldModelSnapshot context = 4;
}
message Verdict {
  enum Status { APPROVED, REJECTED, NEEDS_HUMAN };
  Status status = 1;
  string explanation = 2;
  repeated string violated_rules = 3;
}
```

7.2 City‑wide Sensor Fusion Engine

Implementation:

· Ingests from 100,000+ sensors via Kafka (Plane B) and TTEthernet (Plane A).
· A temporal fusion transformer (TFT) model, trained offline on 5 years of simulated orbital data, produces 10‑second‑ahead predictions for all critical state variables.
· Anomaly detection is performed by a variational autoencoder (VAE) running on FPGA at 100 Hz.
· The world model is a probabilistic graph of all entities (modules, drones, vessels, debris) with covariance estimates.

7.3 Adaptive Resource Scheduler (Booster)

Implementation:

· Uses multi‑agent PPO with centralised training, decentralised execution.
· Each agent controls a group of resources (e.g., a set of photonic cores, a fusion reactor’s electrical output).
· Reward function: minimise energy cost + maximise task throughput + meet thermal constraints.
· Deployed as a ROS 2 node that publishes allocation commands to the HAL power management service.

7.4 Hybrid Photonic‑Electronic AI

Photonic Core:

· Each optical node is a silicon photonic reservoir with 50 virtual nodes created via time‑multiplexing.
· Weights are programmed via thermo‑optic phase shifters; updated monthly from ground‑trained models.
· Control loop: read from fibre‑optic gyroscopes, compute rotation error, output to thruster drivers – all within <500 ns optical‑electronic‑optical conversion.

Neuromorphic:

· Loihi‑2 chips run spiking neural networks (SNNs) for tasks like docking pose estimation and spacewalk gesture recognition.
· SNNs are trained using surrogate gradients offline, then converted to spike‑based execution.

7.5 Quantum Annealer Integration

Integration Pattern:

· The quantum annealer is a shared resource accessed via a job queue.
· Each optimisation problem (e.g., sensor placement, truss routing) is formulated as a QUBO using helper libraries.
· The annealer is housed in a dedicated cryogenic module; results are returned via a REST API (Layer 3).
· Fallback: Classical solvers (simulated annealing, genetic algorithms) run on the HPC cluster if the annealer is offline.

7.6 Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL)

Design:

· Thin C library that provides uniform access to timers, interrupts, DMA, and device registers across ARM, x86, RISC‑V.
· For each platform, a board support package (BSP) implements the HAL primitives.
· All higher layers link against HAL, never directly accessing hardware.
· Enables same binary to run on development workstation (x86) and flight hardware (RISC‑V) after recompilation.

